  leaderboardListText: {fileID: 1586322087}
  allQuestions:
  - fact: Normal color vision uses three types of cone cells
    isTrue: 1
  - fact: Each cone can detect all colors by itself
    isTrue: 0
  - fact: The brain combines information from multiple cones to perceive color
    isTrue: 1
  - fact: ' Color perception happens only in the brain and not in the eye'
    isTrue: 0
  - fact: Deuteranopia affects the cone cells responsible for green light
    isTrue: 1
  - fact: People with Deuteranopia see only black and white
    isTrue: 0
  - fact: Deuteranopia is one of the most common types of color blindness
    isTrue: 1
  - fact: Deuteranopia affects blue-sensitive cone cells
    isTrue: 0
  - fact: Protanopia affects brightness perception
    isTrue: 1
  - fact: ' L-cones do not function properly in Protanopia'
    isTrue: 1
  - fact: Protanopia and Deuteranopia are the same condition
    isTrue: 0
  - fact: People with Protanopia cannot see any colors
    isTrue: 0
  - fact: Tritanopia is a rare type of color blindness
    isTrue: 1
  - fact: Tritanopia affects red-sensitive cone cells
    isTrue: 0
  - fact: Tritanopia can be acquired and is not always genetic
    isTrue: 1
  - fact: People with Tritanopia may confuse blue and yellow colors
    isTrue: 1
